/** @page odbcexceptionsguide Exception Types and Error Handling

    @tableofcontents

    The ODBC driver raises various exceptions to indicate error conditions. Understanding these exceptions helps in writing robust error handling code.

    @section odbc_connection_errors Connection Errors

    |!Exception|!Description|!Common Causes|!Resolution
    |\c ODBC-CONNECTION-ERROR|Connection to the database failed|Invalid credentials, server unavailable, network issues, incorrect connection string|Verify connection string, credentials, network connectivity, and that the database server is running
    |\c ODBC-CONNECTION-HANDLE-ERROR|Cannot allocate a connection handle|Resource exhaustion, ODBC driver issues|Check ODBC driver configuration, restart application
    |\c ODBC-CONNECTION-DEAD-ERROR|The database connection has been lost|Network failure, server restart, idle timeout|Reconnect to the database; consider using connection pooling with health checks
    |\c ODBC-ENV-HANDLE-ERROR|Cannot allocate an environment handle|ODBC driver not installed or misconfigured|Install/reinstall the ODBC driver, check odbcinst.ini configuration
    |\c ODBC-NO-CONNECTION-ERROR|Operation attempted without an open connection|Calling database methods before connecting or after disconnection|Ensure connection is established before executing operations

    @section odbc_statement_errors Statement Errors

    |!Exception|!Description|!Common Causes|!Resolution
    |\c ODBC-EXEC-ERROR|SQL statement execution failed|SQL syntax error, constraint violation, permission denied|Check SQL syntax, verify table/column names, check database permissions
    |\c ODBC-PREPARE-ERROR|Failed to prepare SQL statement|Invalid SQL syntax, referenced objects don't exist|Validate SQL syntax, ensure all referenced tables and columns exist
    |\c ODBC-PARSE-ERROR|Invalid SQL specification|Malformed SQL, invalid placeholder syntax|Check SQL syntax and placeholder formatting
    |\c ODBC-STATEMENT-ALLOC-ERROR|Cannot allocate a statement handle|Resource exhaustion, too many open statements|Close unused statements, check for statement handle leaks
    |\c ODBC-FETCH-ERROR|Error fetching data from result set|Connection lost during fetch, data conversion error|Check connection stability, verify data type compatibility
    |\c ODBC-SELECT-ROW-ERROR|selectRow() returned more than one row|Query returned multiple rows when only one was expected|Add LIMIT 1 to query or refine WHERE clause to ensure single row result

    @section odbc_binding_errors Binding Errors

    |!Exception|!Description|!Common Causes|!Resolution
    |\c ODBC-BIND-ERROR|Invalid parameter binding|Invalid type constant passed to odbc_bind(), type mismatch|Use valid ODBCT_* constants, ensure parameter types match column types
    |\c ODBC-BIND-PLACEHOLDERS-ERROR|Placeholder binding not supported|Calling bindPlaceholders() which is not supported by ODBC driver|Use bindArgs() or bind() instead of bindPlaceholders()

    @section odbc_transaction_errors Transaction Errors

    |!Exception|!Description|!Common Causes|!Resolution
    |\c ODBC-COMMIT-ERROR|Transaction commit failed|Connection lost, database constraint violation during commit|Check connection, verify all data meets constraints, retry transaction
    |\c ODBC-ROLLBACK-ERROR|Transaction rollback failed|Connection lost during rollback|Reconnect to database; the transaction should be automatically rolled back by the server
    |\c ODBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR|Connection lost during active transaction|Network failure, server restart|Reconnect and retry the transaction

    @section odbc_option_errors Option Errors

    |!Exception|!Description|!Common Causes|!Resolution
    |\c ODBC-OPTION-ERROR|Invalid option value|Wrong data type for option, value out of range|Check option documentation for valid types and ranges

    @section odbc_error_handling_example Error Handling Example

    @code{.py}
    try {
        Datasource ds("odbc:user/pass@dsn");

        try {
            ds.exec("INSERT INTO users (id, name) VALUES (%v, %v)", 1, "Test");
            ds.commit();
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # Handle execution errors
            if (ex.err == "ODBC-EXEC-ERROR") {
                printf("SQL execution failed: %s\n", ex.desc);
            }
            ds.rollback();
        }
    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
        # Handle connection errors
        if (ex.err == "ODBC-CONNECTION-ERROR") {
            printf("Could not connect to database: %s\n", ex.desc);
        } else if (ex.err == "ODBC-ENV-HANDLE-ERROR") {
            printf("ODBC driver issue: %s\n", ex.desc);
        }
    }
    @endcode
*/
